-- title:
--   Level-N modular function field equals its full divisor field
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number, assumed non-zero (via the `NeZero N` instance). Inside the field of Laurent series $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, written `LaurentSeries ℚ`, write `jq` for the fixed Laurent series that serves as the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$, and for each non-zero $d$ let `qExpand ℚ d` denote the ring endomorphism of `LaurentSeries ℚ` obtained by multiplying all Hahn-series exponents by $d$, i.e. the substitution $q \mapsto q^{d}$; thus `qExpand ℚ d jq` is the series $j(q^{d})$. The theorem asserts the equality of two intermediate fields of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$: on the one hand `modularFunctionField N`, the subfield generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the two elements `jq` and `qExpand ℚ N jq`, and on the other hand `modularFunctionFieldFull N`, the subfield generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the whole set of divisor expansions $\{\,$`qExpand ℚ d jq`$\,\}$, where $d$ ranges over the non-zero divisors of $N$. Equivalently: every $j(q^{d})$ with $d \mid N$ is a rational function of $j(q)$ and $j(q^{N})$ with rational coefficients, and conversely.
--
--   This is the generation statement for the function field of $X_0(N)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ in its formal $q$-expansion model: the field generated by $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$ already contains all intermediate divisor expansions $j(q^d)$, $d \mid N$. It is used throughout the development of the $q$-expansion model of $X_0(N)$, in particular in the treatment of mod $p$ modular forms and of Hecke operators on fibre models.
